-c004-594d-a48d-9275a75ead43" :question/math-academy-id "q-10495" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the hyperbola $9{y}^{2}-20{x}^{2}=180$ intersects the line $x=6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=6,$ we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{y}^{2}-20{x}^{2} & =180 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-20(6)^{2} & =180 \\\\ 9{y}^{2}-720 & =180 \\\\ {y}^{2}-80 & =20 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =80+20 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =100 \\\\ y & =\\pm 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hyperbola intersects the vertical line $x=6$  at the points $(6,\\pm 10).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-10495/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "395f435d-473f-5dae-b215-2986556d5793"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-10495"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-170556"] :question/problem "Calculate the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ intersects the line $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $x=-1,$ we substitute $x=-1$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. \nSo, the hyperbola does not intersect the vertical line $x=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-10485/selection/0be40a41fff2075dcd7ae30d0e6284ddf7b2ec7e519882e4636a79e6edbe7cee" :answer/id #uuid "8672317d-3771-5265-a89f-7b6f8e99ade1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=\\pm 2"